# Question 1: Advanced Problem-Solving Approach Using Linear and Non-Linear Collections

**Problem Statement:**

An online shopping system must maintain products, quickly search products by ID, and process orders according to priority. Use linear and non-linear collections in Java to manage products and orders efficiently.

**Sample Input:**

```text
5
101 Laptop 55000
102 Mouse 500
103 Keyboard 1500
104 Monitor 12000
105 Headphones 2000
103
3
101 2
102 1
104 3
```

**Sample Output:**

```text
Products:
101 Laptop 55000.0
102 Mouse 500.0
103 Keyboard 1500.0
104 Monitor 12000.0
105 Headphones 2000.0

Search Result:
103 Keyboard 1500.0

Orders Processed by Priority:
Order ID: 102, Priority: 1
Order ID: 101, Priority: 2
Order ID: 104, Priority: 3
```

**Java Code:**

```java
import java.util.*;

class Product {
    int id;
    String name;
    double price;

    Product(int id, String name, double price) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public String toString() {
        return id + " " + name + " " + price;
    }
}

class Order {
    int orderId, priority;

    Order(int orderId, int priority) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        this.priority = priority;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        int n = sc.nextInt();

        // Linear collection: ArrayList
        ArrayList<Product> products = new ArrayList<>();

        // Non-linear collection: HashMap for fast ID search
        HashMap<Integer, Product> productMap = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            int id = sc.nextInt();
            String name = sc.next();
            double price = sc.nextDouble();

            Product p = new Product(id, name, price);
            products.add(p);
            productMap.put(id, p);
        }

        System.out.println("Products:");
        for (Product p : products) {
            System.out.println(p);
        }

        int searchId = sc.nextInt();
        System.out.println("\nSearch Result:");
        if (productMap.containsKey(searchId)) {
            System.out.println(productMap.get(searchId));
        } else {
            System.out.println("Product not found");
        }

        // PriorityQueue processes lower priority numbers first
        PriorityQueue<Order> orders = new PriorityQueue<>(
            (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a.priority, b.priority)
        );

        int m = sc.nextInt();
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            int orderId = sc.nextInt();
            int priority = sc.nextInt();
            orders.add(new Order(orderId, priority));
        }

        System.out.println("\nOrders Processed by Priority:");
        while (!orders.isEmpty()) {
            Order o = orders.poll();
            System.out.println("Order ID: " + o.orderId
                    + ", Priority: " + o.priority);
        }

        sc.close();
    }
}
```

**Collections Used:**

* `ArrayList` – stores products in a linear collection.
* `HashMap` – searches products by ID efficiently.
* `PriorityQueue` – processes orders according to priority, with smaller numbers indicating higher priority.
